[08:26.97]Passage One
[08:28.91]Across organizations and industries, people make a significant effort to learn and adhere to dress codes, etiquette and other codes of behavior. 
[08:41.01]For example, we tend to dress up for job interviews, dates and business meetings. 
[08:47.54]Abiding by social norms, rules and expectations is an attempt to gain social acceptance and status and avoid negative impressions and social disapproval.
[09:00.50]Yet it may be that violating the accepted dress code or social norms may have surprising status benefits.
[09:08.77]Researchers found that under certain conditions, such disobeying can signal more success and competence. 
[09:16.88]Notably, while unintentional violations of social norms can work against people, deliberate violations can lead to perception of higher status. 
[09:28.29]In their research, it is found that shop assistants working in luxury clothes shops assigned greater status to a woman wearing gym clothes than to a woman wearing a fur coat. 
[09:41.02]In another study, students viewed a 45-year-old professor to have higher status when he was wearing a T-shirt as compared to when he was wearing a tie.
[09:52.72]Why is this the case? 
[09:54.53]Disobedience often carries a social cost. 
[09:57.98]As a result, observers tend to infer that a rebellious individual is in such a powerful position that they can risk the social costs of violating norms without the fear of losing their places in the social hierarchy.
[10:14.53]Heightening our status in the eyes of others is clearly an important goal, as it can lead us to be more influential members of our groups and organizations. 
[10:24.79]We often devote great effort to enhancing our status, when it appears that just being willing to deviate from a dress code or other norm may do the trick.
[10:36.67]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[10:42.80]9. Why do people follow social norms, rules and expectations according to the speaker?
[11:06.31]10. What does the speaker say about deliberate violations of social norms?
[11:28.43]11. What do observers think of rebellious individuals?
[11:48.36]Passage Two
[11:50.07]Next month, several chain supermarkets in the UK will stop selling energy drinks to customers under 16. 
[11:58.34]Anyone looking to buy a soft drink with more than 150 milligram of caffeine per liter¡ªa limit targeting drinks like Monster and Red Bull¡ªwill need to present an ID.
[12:10.17]The retailer Waitrose announced its new energy drink restrictions first in early January. 
[12:16.54]Sainsbury's, Morrisons, Lidl, Coop, Asda, Aldi and Tesco followed with their own bans on selling the drink to children, which will all roll out at the beginning of March.
[12:28.26]And while a nationwide soda tax will go into effect in the UK in April, banning so-called ¡°under 16s¡± from buying energy drinks is a voluntary measure that grocery stores have taken up this year.
[12:42.35]It's probably a good idea to pause energy drink sales to children, says Jennifer Temple, a nutrition researcher at the University of Buffalo.
[12:52.41]Small to moderate doses of caffeine haven't had a huge effect on kids in Temple's research, but she's not enthusiastic about supplying children with energy drinks. 
[13:03.96]A major aim of caffeine¡ªpreventing sleep¡ªcan interfere with the rest kids need to grow, think and function. 
[13:11.83]¡°There's no reason why kids need this caffeine,¡± she says. 
[13:15.86]¡°The sleep effects alone are something that we should be mindful of.¡±
[13:20.19]Researchers, teachers and those policy groups count the energy drink bans for kids in grocery stores as a victory, but they're not a perfect, principal solution. 
[13:31.63]The issue with it is that these products will still be bought in convenience stores and other small stores.
[13:39.55]Questions 12 to 15 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[13:44.73]12. What will some chain supermarkets in the UK do next month?
[14:04.61]13. What do we know about the energy drink restrictions?
[14:22.63]14. Why should children be banned to have energy drinks?
[14:41.26]15. Why are the energy drink bans imperfect?
