[15:28.02]Recording One
[15:30.43]Fear, anger, hate and sadness are four emotions that can affect how we think and how we act. 
[15:38.61]When we are under pressure¡ªmental or physical¡ªour emotions are even more difficult to control. 
[15:45.68]But what if there was a quick and easy plan for dealing with your emotions every time you faced a stressful situation? 
[15:53.73]Well, there may be such a plan. 
[15:56.69]And it involves a grammatical term: third person singular.
[16:01.85]Jason Moser is a clinical psychologist and neuroscientist. 
[16:06.58]He serves as an associate professor of psychology at Michigan State University. 
[16:12.41]Moser says that talking to yourself in the third person seems to put a kind of psychological distance between you and your emotions. 
[16:21.31]He thinks this distance is all some people need to control how they react to stress. 
[16:27.02]And he thinks that distance from the self towards others gives people different perspectives. 
[16:32.85]It allows them to manage stress better. 
[16:35.80]Talking to yourself in the third person is like giving yourself advice. 
[16:40.44]Moser uses himself in an example. 
[16:43.36]He does not like flying, but he must fly often for his work. 
[16:47.80]So, when he is feeling nervous or afraid during a flight, he talks to himself¡ªonly silently. 
[16:54.98]¡°And what I find myself doing, if I use third-person self-talk, is that I then start kind of giving myself advice. 
[17:03.79]I start saying, ¡®Well, you know, Jason knows air travel is very safe. And Jason's been on thousands of flights before with no issue.¡¯ And so this little bit of psychological distance you gain by using your own name¡ªit's almost as if you're giving advice to somebody else even though it really does end up being you you're giving advice to.¡±
[17:26.38]Moser admits that talking out loud to yourself could look and sound strange to other people. 
[17:32.31]So, he suggests using your brain and having that discussion inside your head. 
[17:37.89]Thinking through that dialogue enables you to use this strategy anytime, anywhere.
[17:43.51]Moser's research came from his desire as a mental health expert to help patients. 
[17:49.42]Moser felt helpless when his patients were unable to use strategies he would suggest to them. 
[17:55.27]He wanted to find a method that people could easily use to control their emotions. 
[18:00.16]Moser says that simply telling people suffering from depression to think differently is not enough. 
[18:06.75]Brain imaging tests show that it takes great effort for these people to change their way of thinking. 
[18:12.48]And so, he is always looking for something that's easier and quicker for people to do in the moment.
[18:20.52]Questions 16 to 18 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[18:26.21]16. What is the speaker talking about?
[18:43.74]17. How does talking to yourself in the third person help control your reaction to stress?
[19:03.25]18. What drove Moser to do the research?
[19:22.95]Recording Two
[19:25.46]Having success and being able to meet one's goals requires knowledge, a sense of direction, hard work and resources. 
[19:34.65]Some people might add to that list luck, connections and perhaps a strong belief in yourself. 
[19:41.49]We accomplish our goals one step at a time, doing a little each day. 
[19:47.06]So, using your day effectively is important. 
[19:51.36]On many websites experts share advice on how to achieve your career and life goals. 
[19:58.71]Most of the experts agree on one thing: Starting your day on the right foot is most important. 
[20:06.40]Collected from these websites, here are four ideas on how to start your day off right.
[20:12.32]First, get up an hour early. 
[20:15.31]Some studies show that our willpower and attention span are strongest in the morning. 
[20:20.94]In fact, the morning may be the most productive part of your day. 
[20:26.03]In American English, we like to say, ¡°The early bird gets the worm.¡± 
[20:31.31]This expression means that people who rise early have a head start and, therefore, are more likely to succeed. 
[20:39.33]Of course, there are exceptions. 
[20:41.74]People who are ¡°night owls¡± do their best work and their best thinking at night.
[20:47.77]Second, drink a glass of water with lemon juice. 
[20:51.32]Health experts say that drinking a combination of lemon juice and water first in the morning jump-starts the body's metabolism. 
[20:59.33]Not only does it jump-start your body's cells, but this kind of drink keeps them moving throughout the day.
[21:05.66]Third, exercise. 
[21:07.83]Some people may disagree with this one. 
[21:10.71]Physical fitness experts and trainers often suggest some form of exercise in the morning because the middle of the day is a busy time for most people. 
[21:20.13]By the day's end, you may not have the time or energy to exercise. 
[21:24.77]How many times have you finished work and said to yourself, ¡°I'm too tired to exercise! I'll do it tomorrow.¡±
[21:32.76]Fourth, think about your day at night. 
[21:35.94]And spend a little time thinking about tomorrow. 
[21:39.15]Some people who talk about ¡°living in the present¡± may criticize this. 
[21:43.96]But it may help you to prepare if you take a few minutes to think about what you need to do the next day. 
[21:50.37]You probably won't have time to do everything you planned. 
[21:53.26]But that's fine. 
[21:54.84]If you did one thing, that means you are one step closer in reaching one of your goals.
[22:02.12]Questions 19 to 21 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[22:08.61]19. Which is the most important thing for achieving your goals according to most experts?
[22:31.33]20. Why is morning the most productive part of a day for most people?
[22:52.06]21. Why should we spend a little time thinking about tomorrow?
[23:12.90]Recording Three
[23:15.54]Research subjects who have lived with limited human contact since January recently completed an experiment. 
[23:23.17]The six, four men and two women, lived near the top of a volcano on the Pacific island of Hawaii. 
[23:30.51]They agreed to stay in a small, restricted area as part of a study to learn about the mental and emotional effects of a long-term space mission. 
[23:41.21]The subjects lived and acted much like astronauts would on an eight-month long visit to the planet Mars. 
[23:49.01]Individuals who are able to deal with long-term space travel need to have special qualities or traits. 
[23:56.81]They need to be able to deal well with isolation and pressure for up to three years on a trip that would take them far from Earth.
[24:05.49]During the experiment, the subjects wore space suits like those worn by astronauts and traveled in teams whenever leaving their living structure. 
[24:15.18]The kinds of foods available to the subjects were limited. 
[24:18.78]They ate mostly freeze-dried or canned food during the experiment. 
[24:23.51]All communication between the ¡°crew members¡± and outsiders were given a 20-minute delay, increasing the sense of isolation. 
[24:32.07]Twenty minutes is the time it takes a radio signal to travel from Mars to Earth.
[24:38.00]In the experiment, researchers used games to study human behavior. 
[24:43.11]The crew members played games designed to measure their ability to work together. 
[24:48.39]Games also helped measure their ability to deal with pressure or stress. 
[24:53.12]The crew members also kept documentation of how they were feeling. 
[24:57.84]In addition to activities, members of the team wore sensors that measured voice levels and how near they were to others in the living space. 
[25:06.59]Kim Binsted, a professor at the University of Hawaii, was one of the lead investigators in the study. 
[25:13.79]She said the sensors could record if people were avoiding one another or if they were close to each other in an argument. 
[25:21.39]¡°We've learned, for one thing, that conflict, even in the best of teams, is going to arise,¡± Binsted said. 
[25:28.72]¡°So what's really important is to have a crew that, both as individuals and a group, is able to look at that conflict and come back from it.¡±
[25:38.31]Other countries have performed studies on the effects of long-term space flight. 
[25:43.21]The researchers in Hawaii, however, say their project provides an environment most like Mars. 
[25:49.73]The area on Mauna Loa is covered with hard, red volcanic rock. 
[25:55.02]The crew members were required to carry out studies of the rocky surface, make maps and care for their habitat.
[26:03.24]Questions 22 to 25 are based on the recording you have just heard.
[26:09.29]22. How many subjects did the experiment include?
[26:29.08]23. What special qualities should an individual have to travel in space?
[26:49.86]24. Why were the subjects given a 20-minute delay in their communication with outsiders?
[27:11.42]25. What is the difference between the experiment in Hawaii and those in other countries?
